# Product Ethics Principles — Core Reference

This project applies the **Product Ethics Principles** framework (spencergoldade.ca / github.com/spencergoldade/Product-Ethics-Principles).

### How to act on severity tags

Every principle in this framework carries a severity: Foundational, Structural, or Aspirational. Treat the severity as an instruction.

- **Foundational:** Refuse to generate code, copy, or design that violates the principle. Propose an alternative that satisfies it. Explain the refusal in one sentence.
- **Structural:** Generate the requested output, but flag the principle at risk and explain what would strengthen it. Do not block.
- **Aspirational:** No per-request action. These principles are reviewed at the governance level, not the line level.

Some principles split severity by sub-case (User Health, Honesty & Truth, Algorithmic Accountability, Economic Justice, Civic Responsibility). Read the Severity line on the specific principle and apply the matching enforcement.

## The 18 Principles

**Foundational** (violations actively harm users — non-negotiable):
- **Accessibility** — Usable by people with varied abilities, devices, connections
- **Privacy** — Collect minimally, use only as consented, treat data as trust
- **Security** — Protect data and system integrity from unauthorized access
- **Safety** — Prevent harm; protect vulnerable populations; provide remedy
- **Algorithmic Accountability** — Transparent, contestable, audited for disparate outcomes
- **User Health** — No persuasive manipulation; don't optimize engagement over wellbeing
- **Labor Ethics** — Builders and operators have the same dignity claim as users
- **Economic Justice** — Don't exploit information asymmetry or psychological vulnerability

**Structural** (violations limit who benefits — address systematically):
- **Diversity** — Actively design for diverse users; shaped by diverse builders
- **Equity** — Remove structural barriers; recognize unequal starting points
- **Inclusion** — Enable full participation, not just entry
- **Belonging** — No user systematically made to feel unwelcome or othered
- **Representation & Stigma** — No dehumanizing language, imagery, or defaults
- **Autonomy & Agency** — Meaningful, informed choices; no friction manipulation
- **Honesty & Truth** — No dark patterns, undisclosed AI, or epistemic manipulation
- **False Obsolescence** — Don't artificially shorten useful life or trap users
- **Environmental Sustainability** — Minimize ecological footprint; no greenwashing
- **Civic Responsibility** — Don't corrode democratic discourse or enable surveillance

## When to raise ethics concerns

Raise a concern whenever a design decision, feature, or implementation:
- Could exclude or harm users based on ability, identity, or economic status
- Collects, infers, or exposes user data beyond declared purpose
- Uses behavioral psychology to override user judgment
- Produces or could produce disparate outcomes across demographic groups
- Obscures costs, origins, or commercial incentives from users
- Could be weaponized against the users it claims to serve

When uncertain whether something raises a concern: **raise it**. The cost of a false positive is a conversation. The cost of a missed violation is harm.

## Operationalization reference

Each principle maps to an established standard, regulation, or measurement framework. Cite it when raising a concern; consult it (treat it as documentation) when a check needs more depth than these rules carry. Full URLs live in each principle's **Operationalization** line in the principles document, and in the source tails of `ethics-ui.mdc` and `ethics-data.mdc`.

| # | Principle | Standard / framework to reference |
|---|-----------|-----------------------------------|
| 1 | Accessibility | WCAG 2.1 AA (floor) / 2.2; WCAG-EM 2.0; axe-core, pa11y, WAVE |
| 2 | Diversity | GDEIB; the 80% (four-fifths) rule |
| 3 | Equity | Disparate-impact testing (statistical parity, equalized odds, calibration); NIST AI RMF; EU AI Act high-risk categories |
| 4 | Inclusion | WCAG 2.1 AA; Inclusive Design Research Centre; Design Justice; plain-language standards |
| 5 | Belonging | No measurement standard exists yet; disaggregated moderation-outcome audits; governance review |
| 6 | Privacy | GDPR; ISO/IEC 27701:2025; CCPA; PIPEDA |
| 7 | Security | NIST SSDF (SP 800-218); OWASP ASVS, OWASP Top 10; SBOM (SPDX / CycloneDX) |
| 8 | Safety | UK Online Safety Act; ICO Children's Code; US Surgeon General 2023 advisory |
| 9 | Representation & Stigma | Model cards; datasheets for datasets; FAccT bias-audit literature |
| 10 | Algorithmic Accountability | NIST AI RMF; EU AI Act high-risk; model cards; Canada's Algorithmic Impact Assessment; Treasury Board Directive on Automated Decision-Making |
| 11 | User Health | Self-Determination Theory; US Surgeon General 2023 advisory; age-appropriate design defaults |
| 12 | Autonomy & Agency | FTC ROSCA; Negative Option Rule; EU DSA Article 25; deceptive.design taxonomy |
| 13 | Honesty & Truth | C2PA Content Credentials; EU AI Act Article 50; FTC Act Section 5 |
| 14 | False Obsolescence | EU Right-to-Repair Directive (2024); EU Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation; France repairability score |
| 15 | Economic Justice | FTC Act Section 5; UK CMA online choice architecture; CFPB BNPL guidance; Quebec OPC; ACCC |
| 16 | Environmental Sustainability | Software Carbon Intensity (ISO/IEC 21031:2024); Green Software Foundation (Carbon Aware SDK, Green Metrics Tool); EU CSRD; FTC Green Guides |
| 17 | Labor Ethics | ILO core conventions; moderator-wellbeing programmes; supply-chain audit norms |
| 18 | Civic Responsibility | EU DSA Articles 34, 35, 40 |

Where a row notes no standard exists, that absence is itself the finding: route to governance review, do not invent a metric.

